---
import PageLayout from '@layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/config/site';

const lastUpdated = 'June 16, 2025';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Corrections Policy"
  description="How to report errors on AstraLumen and how we investigate, correct, and acknowledge factual mistakes in our science content."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Corrections Policy' }]}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="hero-icon" aria-hidden="true">✏️</span>
      <h1>Corrections Policy</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">We are committed to accuracy. When we get something wrong, we fix it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose legal-prose">
      <p>
        {SITE.name} strives for the highest standards of scientific accuracy. Despite our
        rigorous editorial and review process, errors can occur. When they do, we correct
        them promptly and transparently. This policy explains how to report errors and
        how we handle corrections.
      </p>

      <h2>What Qualifies for Correction</h2>
      <p>We correct verifiable factual errors, including but not limited to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Incorrect scientific data, measurements, or statistics</li>
        <li>Misattributed quotes, discoveries, or historical events</li>
        <li>Outdated information that no longer reflects scientific consensus</li>
        <li>Broken or incorrect links to primary sources</li>
        <li>Errors in calculations within our science tools</li>
        <li>Misspelled scientific terms, species names, or proper nouns</li>
        <li>Misleading or ambiguous phrasing that could cause misunderstanding</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What We Typically Do Not Correct</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Differences of scientific opinion where legitimate debate exists among experts</li>
        <li>Stylistic preferences or editorial tone (unless clarity is affected)</li>
        <li>Requests to remove accurate but uncomfortable information</li>
        <li>Subjective interpretations clearly presented as such</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to Report an Error</h2>
      <p>If you believe you have found an error, please contact us with the following information:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Article URL</strong> — the direct link to the page containing the error</li>
        <li><strong>Description</strong> — a clear explanation of what is incorrect</li>
        <li><strong>Correct information</strong> — what the accurate information should be</li>
        <li><strong>Source</strong> — a link to a credible source supporting the correction (peer-reviewed paper, official agency, etc.)</li>
        <li><strong>Your contact information</strong> (optional) — so we can follow up if needed</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Report via Contact Form</h3>
      <p>
        Use our <a href="/contact">contact form</a> and select "Report a Correction" as the subject.
        This is the fastest way to reach our editorial team.
      </p>

      <h3>Report via Email</h3>
      <p>
        Send correction requests to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> with
        "Correction Request" in the subject line.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Correction Process</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Acknowledgment</strong> — We aim to acknowledge receipt within 2 business days</li>
        <li><strong>Investigation</strong> — Our editorial team and subject-matter experts verify the reported error against primary sources</li>
        <li><strong>Correction</strong> — If confirmed, we update the article promptly</li>
        <li><strong>Notification</strong> — For significant corrections, we may add a correction note at the top or bottom of the article</li>
        <li><strong>Follow-up</strong> — If you provided contact information, we will notify you of the outcome</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Types of Corrections</h2>
      <h3>Minor Corrections</h3>
      <p>
        Typos, broken links, and small factual errors are corrected silently. The article's
        "last updated" date is revised to reflect the change.
      </p>

      <h3>Major Corrections</h3>
      <p>
        Significant factual errors that could mislead readers receive a visible correction note.
        The note includes the date of correction and a brief description of what was changed.
        Example:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <strong>Correction (June 16, 2025):</strong> An earlier version of this article
        stated that Jupiter has 95 moons. The correct count, per NASA's latest tally, is 97.
        The article has been updated accordingly.
      </blockquote>

      <h3>Retractions</h3>
      <p>
        In rare cases where an article contains fundamental errors that cannot be adequately
        corrected through revision, we may retract the article and redirect readers to
        accurate alternative resources.
      </p>

      <h2>Update vs. Correction</h2>
      <p>
        Not every content change is a correction. We regularly update articles to reflect
        new scientific discoveries, mission results, and expanded coverage. Routine updates
        that add new information (without fixing errors) are noted with an updated date but
        do not receive a correction notice.
      </p>

      <h2>Our Commitment</h2>
      <p>
        We view corrections not as failures but as essential to maintaining trust with our
        readers. Science is a self-correcting process, and so is science journalism. Thank you
        for helping us keep {SITE.name} accurate and reliable.
      </p>

      <p class="policy-footer">
        <em>Last updated: {lastUpdated}</em>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .page-hero {
    padding: var(--space-3xl) 0 var(--space-xl);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .hero-icon {
    font-size: 4rem;
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .hero-lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    max-width: 600px;
    margin: var(--space-md) auto 0;
  }

  .legal-prose {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-4xl);
  }

  .legal-prose ul,
  .legal-prose ol {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .legal-prose li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }

  .policy-footer {
    margin-top: var(--space-3xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-xl);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }
</style>
